Problem 2

The successful businessman Ivan Mikhailovich decided to please his growing child with a New Year trip to warm countries, to which the flight by plane took a long 8 hours. While the plane was climbing, Dad told his son

that the fuel consumption of their plane at take-off is $14040\ \mathrm{kg/h}$, and this value is greater than in flight at constant altitude and constant speed;

that the efficiency of the engines at take-off is about $12\%$;

that the plane is equipped with four engines with a thrust of $127.4\ \mathrm{kN}$ each;

that the mass of the fully loaded and fuelled plane is $208$ tonnes;

and, finally, that the specific heat of combustion of aviation fuel is $43\ \mathrm{MJ/kg}$.

The narration was interrupted by the pilot's announcement that the plane was flying at an altitude of $8230\ \mathrm{m}$ at a speed of $936\ \mathrm{km/h}$. The tired Ivan Mikhailovich concluded: "And now, son, so as not to relax from your studies too early and not to get bored, choose the necessary data from those I told you and calculate how many seconds the climb of the plane lasted." Help Ivan Mikhailovich's son cope with Dad's task.

The decrease of the plane's mass during take-off may be neglected. Take the acceleration of free fall to be $g=10\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$.

Problem 3

A cylindrical vessel of base area $S$ contains $\nu$ moles of an ideal monatomic gas, separated from the surroundings by a weightless piston. The coefficient of friction between the piston and the walls of the vessel is $\mu$. In the initial state the gas has volume $V_0$, temperature $T_0$ and pressure equal to the pressure of the surroundings. When the gas is heated slowly, the piston begins to move when the temperature of the gas rises to the value $1.2\,T_0$. After the temperature $2T_0$ is reached, the heating is stopped and the gas cools down to the initial temperature $T_0$.

Find

1) the final volume of the gas;

2) the amount of heat supplied to the gas during its heating;

3) the magnitude of the reaction force exerted by the walls of the vessel on the piston.
